al cluster member. Outcome (index 3): the transfer future reports success (no error, 0.315 ms), the named target is a Follower at term 4 and never Leader, a transfer-flagged campaign from the non-voter is visible at term 3, and the incumbent's own campaign at term 4 follows, so the incumbent's term was pushed up and it had to win a new election. Recovery (index 4): one leader exists again about 513 ms after the call. Control (indices 0 and 1): the same call to a voter target succeeded and that target became Leader. Compared with the first run of this fixed experiment the decisive elements recur - a successful future, no leadership for the named target, a term-advancing campaign by the non-voter that forces the voters to step down, and eventual recovery - while one element differs with scheduling: there another voter became Leader at term 4, here the deposed incumbent regained leadership itself at term 4, which is why its sampled state reads Leader in this receipt. That difference does not affect the finding, which is about what the caller receives and about the incumbent being forced to re-elect; the sampled term of the voters advanced from 2 to 4 and the incumbent's campaign is visible in the vote stream. Interpretation: the predicate 'the reported outcome reproduces under a different schedule' holds for the elements the finding rests on, and the varying element is recorded rather than hidden.\\\",\n  \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n   \\\"The post-deposition leader identity is schedule-dependent (the incumbent here, another voter in the first run), so this receipt shows a re-election rather than a lasting vacancy; the harm is the unnecessary deposition and the misleading success, not an unrepaired cluster.\\\",\n   \\\"The reproduction uses the same staging, library build and in-memory timings, so it demonstrates schedule robustness rather than independence from the harness.\\\",\n   \\\"In this run the incumbent held leadership again by the time of the sample, so a re